    {
      "id": "bkHC",
      "code_hash": "831a6eacde0bb4b6de4ef23ee6042166",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": "\n    <span><p style=\"font-size: 24px;\">Reactivity has restrictions</p></span><br>\n    </p>The jupyter notebook had two cells that initialized x. This it not \n    allowed in marimo. If two cells both define the same global variable (in this case \n    x), marimo cannot guarantee which definition will be used when a third cell reads \n    it \u2014 the output would depend on the order in which the defining cells run. This \n    could lead to:<br>\n<h2>My Ordered List</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Hidden state (unknown which value is active)</li>\n<li>Hidden bugs (unexpected behavior when rerunning)</li>\n<li>Non-reproducible results (over 96% of Jupyter notebooks on GitHub fail reproducibility tests) docs.marimo.io.</li>\n</ol>\n"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
